    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d633">

                <pb facs="0475" n="471" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg471"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115 Carolina Leaf Tobacco Company/9:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-09-14T10:00:00+08:00">September 14, 1938—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 14—7:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1219. My 1149, August 22, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="86" xml:id="d633-f1"/> and 272 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">872</hi>], June 20, 6 p.m., and Department’s 475, August
                    6, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="87" xml:id="d633-f2"/> in regard to Carolina Leaf Tobacco
                    Company. I have just received a letter from the Acting Japanese Consul General
                    stating that the Japanese authorities are firmly convinced, after careful
                    consideration, that the decision to seize the tobacco is “right and therefore
                    final”.</p>
                <pb facs="0476" n="472" xml:id="pg472"/>
                <p>The letter continues, after reference to previous correspondence, <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#flush">“All the hogsheads of tobacco scrutinized in the
                            godown of the Joint Savings Society were used by the Chinese troops in
                            place of sand bags to cover themselves from attack. In view of this and
                            of the fact that the godown in question had been occupied by the Chinese
                            troops since August, 1937, it is established that the said hogsheads of
                            tobacco had been appropriated by the Chinese troops before they were put
                            to military use. Upon appropriation by the enemy the goods ceased to be
                            neutral private property and became public property of the enemy, and
                            consequently subject to confiscation under the rules of war.</p>
                        <p>In these circumstances I regret that the Japanese authorities are not in
                            a position either to return the goods or accept any claim for
                            damages.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>This seems to be significant as voicing a specific claim to the operation of the
                    rules of war without a declaration of war.</p>
                <p>The Department’s instructions are requested.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping and Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
